from fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException
from pydantic import BaseModel

app = FastAPI()

class User(BaseModel):
    id: int
    username: str
    password: str

class Project(BaseModel):
    id: int
    name: str

class Label(BaseModel):
    id: int
    name: str

class Comment(BaseModel):
    id: int
    text: str

class Task(BaseModel):
    id: int
    title: str
    project_id: int
    labels: list[int]
    comments: list[int]

@app.post("/login")
def login(user: User):
    # Simple auth example
    if user.username == "admin" and user.password == "password":
        return {"token": "example_token"}
    else:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=401, detail="Invalid username or password")

@app.get("/projects")
def get_projects():
    # Return a list of projects
    return [{"id": 1, "name": "Project 1"}, {"id": 2, "name": "Project 2"}]

@app.get("/labels")
def get_labels():
    # Return a list of labels
    return [{"id": 1, "name": "Label 1"}, {"id": 2, "name": "Label 2"}]

@app.get("/comments")
def get_comments():
    # Return a list of comments
    return [{"id": 1, "text": "Comment 1"}, {"id": 2, "text": "Comment 2"}]

@app.get("/tasks")
def get_tasks():
    # Return a list of tasks
    return [{"id": 1, "title": "Task 1", "project_id": 1, "labels": [1], "comments": [1]}]
```

## Explanation
We define a FastAPI app and create models for User, Project, Label, Comment, and Task using Pydantic's BaseModel. We then create API endpoints for login, getting projects, labels, comments, and tasks. The login endpoint checks the username and password, and returns a token if they are correct. The other endpoints return lists of the respective data.

## Required Libraries
No extra libraries needed beyond FastAPI and Pydantic, which are already imported in the code.
